module fluorescence

// Reagents from Jebrail & Wheeler, Anal. Chem. 2009, 81, 330-335
// Protocol instance shown here: BSA (50 mg/mL) precipitated with 20% TCA,
// rinsed with chloroform/ACN (70/30 v/v), resolubilized in 100 mM borate
// with 1% SDS, and quantified via a fluorescamine fluorescence assay
// (Table 1 / Figure 1b / Figure 3 of the paper).

manifest bsa_sample
manifest tca_precipitant
manifest rinse_solution
manifest resolubilization_buffer
manifest fluorescamine_reagent

instructions:

// Step 1: Precipitation
// Sample and precipitant droplets are merged on the extraction electrode
// and incubated at room temperature (~5 min) until protein precipitates.
sample = dispense 140 units of bsa_sample
tca = dispense 140 units of tca_precipitant
a = mix sample with tca for 60s
heat a at 23c for 5m

// Supernatant is actuated away to waste, leaving precipitate adhered
// to the extraction electrode surface.
dispose a

// Step 2: Rinsing
// Three sequential rinse droplets are driven across the extraction
// electrode to waste to remove impurities from the precipitate.
repeat 3 times {
    rinse = dispense 315 units of rinse_solution
    heat rinse at 23c for 10s
    dispose rinse
}

// Step 3: Resolubilization
// A resolubilization buffer droplet is dispensed and driven to the
// extraction electrode to dissolve the washed precipitate. An optional
// bake step (95C, 5 min) can be used prior to resolubilization to
// remove residual solvent.
resolub = dispense 315 units of resolubilization_buffer
heat resolub at 95c for 5m

// Step 4: Quantification
// Resolubilized protein is reacted with fluorescamine, which becomes
// fluorescent upon reacting with primary amines on the protein, enabling
// quantification of % recovery relative to an unextracted control.
fluor = dispense 13 units of fluorescamine_reagent
b = mix resolub with fluor for 60s

recovery_reading = detect fluorescence on b for 2m
dispose b
